(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-pigmented-skin-lesions-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pigmented Skin Lesions Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old man has several uniformly brown round macules 3 to 4 mm across, some on the forearms and some on the buttocks, unchanged through the winter. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lentigines","Ephelides","Dysplastic naevi","Blue naevi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas and do not fade. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with naevus spilus is found to have a vascular anomaly on further examination. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Naevus spilus can be associated with anomalies of vascular, central nervous system or connective tissue origin","The vascular anomaly is unrelated, since naevus spilus has no described systemic associations at all","The vascular anomaly indicates neurocutaneous melanosis and brain imaging is needed","The vascular anomaly indicates a familial cancer syndrome and genetic testing is needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion itself rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with red hair and fair skin has small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across over her nose and cheeks. Her mother says they are darker every summer and almost invisible by February. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Naevus spilus","Ephelides","Lentigo simplex","Solar lentigines"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the seasonal fading is what separates them from lentigines, which do not fade. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has a sharply circumscribed jet-black papule 5 mm across on the thigh, with faint blue-grey shading at one edge. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A dysplastic naevus of the lower extremity","Pigmented spindle cell naevus, also called Reed naevus","A blue naevus of the lower extremity or the thigh","Spitz naevus of the lower extremity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — commonest in the thirties, females more than males, on the lower extremities and mainly the thigh. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman notices she has fewer moles than she did in her thirties. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Numbers of common acquired naevi peak in the thirties and decline afterwards","Numbers of naevi continue to rise throughout life, so some have been missed","Sun protection over the years has caused the existing naevi to fade away","Naevi that disappear have usually transformed into an unrecognised melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they develop slowly after birth, enlarge, stabilise and then regress. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has a cluster of small light brown macules grouped closely together on one forearm. Which term describes this pattern?","choices":["Agminated lentigines","Generalised lentigo","Acral lentigo","Lentigo simplex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a grouping of small light brown macules, one of the named lentigo types. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"The parents of a child with a large congenital naevus on the back ask whether it can be removed. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["It should never be excised, since surgery on a congenital naevus increases the risk of melanoma","It should be treated with cryotherapy or laser rather than surgery in all young children","It depends on melanoma risk, cosmesis and function; the goal is to remove as much as possible while preserving function","It must be excised completely before school age in every case, regardless of any graft considerations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — where there is little skin for a graft site, observation may be the better option. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 29-year-old has depigmented patches over roughly three per cent of her body surface, mostly on her hands and around the mouth. Which first-line approach fits?","choices":["Tissue or cellular grafting as the definitive treatment","Psoralen plus ultraviolet A therapy in preference to narrow-band ultraviolet B phototherapy","Topical therapy, with calcineurin inhibitors useful where steroids cannot be applied such as face and neck","Narrow-band ultraviolet B phototherapy as first line"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy suits under five per cent involvement, ideally combined with phototherapy. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman is booked for intense pulsed light treatment of her freckles. What must she be told to expect?","choices":["The result is permanent once a full course has been completed","Her freckles will darken before they fade, over about six weeks","She will need lifelong monthly maintenance treatments","The lesions can relapse, so this is not necessarily permanent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck says explicitly that lesions can relapse after light or laser treatment. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 24-year-old with red hair and widespread freckling across her nose and cheeks asks what can be done about them. Which management does the deck support, and which must be avoided?","choices":["Sun protection, patient education and topical depigmenting agents; cryotherapy is NOT used","Cryotherapy to each individual lesion, with sun protection counselling afterwards","Surgical excision of the most prominent lesions","No treatment of any kind is available or appropriate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy is difficult because of the size of the lesions, so intense pulsed light or lasers are preferred. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a 7 mm naevus with an indistinct border and a pebbly surface, with tan and brown areas within it, on the upper back. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Serology","Biopsy","Dermoscopy","Wood's lamp"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic melanocytic naevus is diagnosed histologically. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with dysplastic naevi has one lesion that her partner says has changed shape over three months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure her, since dysplastic naevi are benign and do not change at all","Biopsy that lesion, since all changing or developing lesions are biopsied","Photograph it and review the appearance again in twelve months' time","Excise all of her naevi, since the risk rises with the number present"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — excision follows where there is concern for melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has several lesions with the appearance of Spitz naevi. Which additional consideration does this raise?","choices":["Multiple lesions indicate that neurocutaneous melanosis is likely present","Multiple lesions define dysplastic naevus syndrome in a child of this age","Multiple lesions suggest an inherited disorder such as LAMB or myxoma syndrome","Multiple lesions can be associated with a familial cancer syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — diagnosis is by biopsy or wide excision, and management is by excision. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with stable vitiligo that has not changed in four years asks about surgery. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Surgery is never used in vitiligo, since the grafted skin depigments in the same way","Surgery is offered first line in all patients, ahead of phototherapy and topical therapy","Surgery is offered only where disease is actively spreading across the body surface","Tissue or cellular grafting is an option, since surgery is reserved for highly stable disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — his four stable years are exactly the criterion. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with numerous solar lentigines asks whether the birth control she took for years contributed. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Birth control use is unrelated, since solar lentigines are caused by sun exposure and nothing else","Birth control use is the principal cause, and stopping it will make the lesions fade","Birth control use raises the risk of melanoma within these lesions specifically","Birth control use is among the named associations, alongside older age, sun damage, ephelides and tanning"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 90% of people have solar lentigines by the age of fifty. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 2-year-old with a large congenital melanocytic naevus is being considered for surgery, but the surgeon reports very little uninvolved skin available for grafting. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Use cryotherapy in place of excision, since it needs no donor skin for grafting","Defer any decision until the child reaches adolescence and then reassess it","Observation may be the better option, since graft site availability is part of the decision","Proceed with excision regardless, since melanoma risk always outweighs graft considerations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — surgery is ideal where feasible, but the decision depends on every patient. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"The parents of a child with a very large congenital naevus ask what support exists. What does the deck point them towards, and what shapes the plan?","choices":["Counselling and support groups, with management balancing melanoma risk, cosmetic outcome and preserved function","Counselling alone, since surgery is contraindicated in large naevi","Immediate referral for total surgical excision, regardless of whether a graft site is available","No specific support is described beyond routine dermatology follow-up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the goal is removing as much as possible while preserving function and improving appearance. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man of dark-skinned Asian background has facial papules typical of dermatosis papulosa nigrans and asks whether they will worsen. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["They are benign and best left untreated, with removal available if he wants it for cosmetic reasons","They will progress to melanoma if untreated, so excision of every lesion is advised now","They will resolve on their own within two years if sun protection is used consistently","They are contagious to close contacts, so household members should be examined too"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — excision, curettage or laser are the options, avoiding cryotherapy. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 63-year-old asks about the flat brown patches on the backs of her hands. They are asymptomatic and unchanged. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Excisional biopsy of the largest lesion to exclude melanoma","No treatment is necessary; cosmetic removal by cryotherapy or quality-switched laser only if she wishes","Cryotherapy to all of the lesions, since solar lentigines are considered to be premalignant","Topical fluorouracil as field therapy across both hands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treatment is elective, not indicated. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with facial papules typical of dermatosis papulosa nigrans has one lesion that looks different from the rest. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat all the lesions with cryotherapy and review the atypical one afterwards","Reassure him, since every lesion in this condition is benign without exception","Arrange dermoscopy of the whole face and repeat it again in twelve months","Biopsy that lesion, since biopsy is indicated where the diagnosis is uncertain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is otherwise clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has vitiligo affecting roughly 20% of his body surface. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid alone applied twice daily to all the affected areas","Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented areas","PUVA phototherapy, which is preferred to narrow band ultraviolet B here","Phototherapy with narrow band ultraviolet B, ideally combined with topical therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred over PUVA, which raises skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with freckles across her cheeks asks what will reduce them. Which is the most appropriate treatment plan?","choices":["Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy given three times weekly over a five week course","Sun protection and counselling first, then topical depigmenting agents, with intense pulsed light or laser if needed","Cryotherapy to each lesion with five to ten second freezes, repeated over several sessions as needed","Excision with negative margins for the largest lesions, with observation of the remainder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids and botanicals are the topical options. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with extensive vitiligo says the condition troubles him socially far more than physically. Which aspect of care does the deck single out alongside repigmentation?","choices":["Psychological intervention, alongside cosmetic and non-traditional therapies","Screening the whole family for autoimmune thyroid disease at diagnosis","Annual skin cancer surveillance for every patient","Immediate referral for surgical grafting in all patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is listed as part of management rather than as an afterthought. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with numerous solar lentigines asks what her lesions mean for her health. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The lesions indicate an underlying internal malignancy and warrant an urgent systemic screening of the whole body","The lesions will fade over the coming winter if she avoids further exposure to the sun","The lesions themselves are benign, but they mark sun damage that is also associated with actinic keratosis and skin cancers","The lesions are premalignant and each one carries a measurable chance of becoming a melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the association includes squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma and melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with several lentigines on the hands asks whether they need treating. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Sun protection alone will make the lesions fade over the course of the following winter","No treatment is necessary; cosmetic removal by cryotherapy or quality-switched laser is an option if she prefers","Excision with clear margins is needed on every single lesion, because of the risk of transformation","Topical depigmenting agents are the only option, since cryotherapy cannot be used at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and the lesions are benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man asks whether treating his solar lentigines will reduce his skin cancer risk. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Treating them has no cosmetic or medical value, so no intervention should be offered at all","Treating them is essential, since untreated lesions progress to melanoma within a decade","Treating them is cosmetic; reducing risk depends on sun protection and surveillance for other lesions","Treating them removes the precancerous cells and reduces the risk substantially over time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lesions mark sun damage rather than causing the cancers associated with it. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel warty and look pasted onto the skin. She is worried they are cancers. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They are dysplastic naevi requiring excision because of the risk of melanoma","They are solar lentigines requiring cryotherapy for cosmetic improvement","They are benign seborrheic keratoses, diagnosed clinically, and management is supportive","They are premalignant lesions requiring biopsy of each one before any reassurance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they are common in older adults and easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"The parents of a child with a very large congenital naevus feel isolated and overwhelmed. Which support does the lecture name?","choices":["Referral to palliative care for early goals-of-care discussion with the family","Counselling and support groups for families and patients with large naevi","Referral to a genetics service for testing of both parents and the child","Referral to psychiatry for management of the parental distress specifically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is named alongside the surgical and observational decision. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with a stable 5 mm blue macule on the scalp asks whether it needs removing. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Excision is required now, since blue naevi have a high malignant potential","Cryotherapy is appropriate, since it clears deeply pigmented dermal lesions","Biopsy is required now, since all blue naevi are diagnosed histologically","Observation is appropriate, with biopsy or excision only if changes are noted"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — small lesions are diagnosed clinically. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has one seborrheic keratosis on the back that has become itchy and inflamed where his belt rubs. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical depigmenting agents applied over several months","Observation alone, since no treatment exists for these","Cryotherapy, though the lesion may recur afterwards","Excision with negative margins to prevent it recurring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — asymptomatic lesions need no treatment at all. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
